Workflow executed (analysis_pipeline.py) -- 9 steps
---------------------------------------------------
1. Loaded 4 data assets: temporal_trends (12 rows x 3 cols), invasion_success
   (81 x 19), invaded_niche (632 x 37), phylogenetic_tree (81-tip Newick).
   Two CSV files use tab separators; corrected at load time.

2. Per-pathway NB2 GLM grid (canonical analysis, 27 cells = 9 urban snapshots
   x 3 pathway counts): for each cell fit
       n_pathway ~ focal_urban + matched-year/buffer cropland + elevation +
                   distance.stream + distance.road + climate (T, rainfall,
                   radiation) + (habitat FE)
   with HC0 robust standard errors. Best dispersion alpha selected over a grid
   {(0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0)} by AIC. Benjamini-Hochberg FDR
   applied across the 27 cells.

3. Sensitivity refit: same 27 cells without habitat fixed effects.

4. Dispersion diagnostics: Poisson-vs-NB2 LR test, AIC comparison ->
   confirms NB2 over Poisson for Gardening and Unintentional; for
   AgriForest no overdispersion detected (small-N).

5. Compositional ALR (additive-log-ratio) supplementary: AgriForest as
   reference category; restricted to non-empty plots (n=191); pseudocount
   0.5 to avoid log(0). OLS on each of alr_gard and alr_unint for each
   of the 9 urban predictors.

6. Change-variable NB models: n_pathway ~ progressive / regressive /
   no.changes proportions for the 1956-2009 and 1993-2009 windows at the
   50m buffer, plus the usual control set. HC0 robust SE.

7. Ancillary species-level: pathway-level mean urban land cover across
   hosting plots, plus family-clustered OLS sanity check at species level.

8. Descriptive temporal and habitat crosstab pivots for the narrative,
   including the 1985-2019 Gardening surge and the urban-class plot counts.

9. All artifacts written to /workspace/artifacts/, including the hypothesis
   paragraph and this workflow summary.

Reproducibility / environment
-----------------------------
- Python 3.12, statmodels (NB2 GLM with HC0), scipy (LR test, Kruskal-Wallis),
  pandas (data wrangling), numpy (numeric ops).
- matplotlib/seaborn/sklearn not available in the runtime; numeric pivot
  tables and ASCII summaries are used instead of figures.
- Newick: parsed with a hand-rolled regex to confirm 81 tips matching the
  phylo.tip.label column of invasion_success_pathways (81 species, 33 families).
